 "question-d9482400-abcf-59ec-ba2d-fb58a274b62b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161780 :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d054c7daaaeefd4cdf820d49d04d6a0bd544f09e356b859b6291ff7d4588e19.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=0,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_3=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector \n\n![](images/98/980726b042df523d4c12352fcde141e8c39701a23d72c2815165517dc8ab6949.svg)\n\nwhere $\\| \\mathbf{v}_3 \\| = \\sqrt{6}.$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_3$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/ca/caa7100961d28805820530d11708ac6dee747a75b858494d84fcd4c6e5c5d1fb.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161989 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9e34d3f6-344a-52de-9e80-5526338f9855" "question-2f400860-7ffc-5147-852c-7cca9efa6b2f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161781 :question/problem "![](images/da/dacf2240e98ef232fd9b5cd8d12baddb808e6f30c2956d6545370095346c9f22.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D=P^T \\! AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, find the first and second columns of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D=P^T \\! AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=-1,$ and $\\lambda_3=8.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/b9/b90c25fdfb269ffd1c6fe30dc77f9142986f1b72adc23b5e71e2d9a4182795ab.svg) and ![](images/f1/f1313b31d2bce0d0358f5a9255aeb04f13cecfc0d65ae1d1028024ee5a029bc8.svg)\n\nNotice that eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/bf/bf1b9a7dbbca5e1df2e7acb7cf62ef04e35791252bbd8020715c4d7bde23c577.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{5}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{e}_1$ and $\\mathbf{e}_2$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-94cf2153-a12b-5a66-9939-44bd440747ca" :question/id #uuid "94cf2153-a12b-5a66-9939-44bd440747ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-140829" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c3a999b2-e6df-50f1-b9e9-4b5772ed9a89"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c3a999b2-e6df-50f1-b9e9-4b5772ed9a89" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3a999b2-e6df-50f1-b9e9-4b5772ed9a89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-19369627-6a7f-57a0-bdc7-627b463790c3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3cd90262-baea-5f31-bc98-cf29ad858555" "answer-35d5ccf2-30c9-5cbf-acc9-77d3639bf40d" "answer-5fdd90f8-f543-5147-b91e-eb93bde7c762" "answer-88c2827e-293f-5e84-bd99-f7c70cbaee50" "answer-19369627-6a7f-57a0-bdc7-627b463790c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cd90262-baea-5f31-bc98-cf29ad858555" :answer/id #uuid "3cd90262-baea-5f31-bc98-cf29ad858555" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35d5ccf2-30c9-5cbf-acc9-77d3639bf40d" :answer/id #uuid "35d5ccf2-30c9-5cbf-acc9-77d3639bf40d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fdd90f8-f543-5147-b91e-eb93bde7c762" :answer/id #uuid "5fdd90f8-f543-5147-b91e-eb93bde7c762" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88c2827e-293f-5e84-bd99-f7c70cbaee50" :answer/id #uuid "88c2827e-293f-5e84-bd99-f7c70cbaee50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19369627-6a7f-57a0-bdc7-627b463790c3" :answer/id #uuid "19369627-6a7f-57a0-bdc7-627b463790c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-20edcd99-4d6d-5145-9c47-d9e4447f46d0" :question/id #uuid "20edcd99-4d6d-5145-9c47-d9e4447f46d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94252" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,∗\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f440a145-0bf3-5ff0-b0c3-e81bf8ba0f0c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f440a145-0bf3-5ff0-b0c3-e81bf8ba0f0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "f440a145-0bf3-5ff0-b0c3-e81bf8ba0f0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3081f2ae-c1c3-5db2-ae1a-ba7cab09ec92" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b962a40a-0a66-5d50-95c4-303ca8b2e58e" "answer-bdf02e3a-145e-54f5-aabf-cb0fe13d64ec" "answer-3081f2ae-c1c3-5db2-ae1a-ba7cab09ec92" "answer-a4482997-246f-5013-aaa1-f55168fbc114" "answer-e218adf9-787a-5fa5-9fc5-f2f5a38f2395"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b962a40a-0a66-5d50-95c4-303ca8b2e58e" :answer/id #uuid "b962a40a-0a66-5d50-95c4-303ca8b2e58e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdf02e3a-145e-54f5-aabf-cb0fe13d64ec" :answer/id #uuid "bdf02e3a-145e-54f5-aabf-cb0fe13d64ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3081f2ae-c1c3-5db2-ae1a-ba7cab09ec92" :answer/id #uuid "3081f2ae-c1c3-5db2-ae1a-ba7cab09ec92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4482997-246f-5013-aaa1-f55168fbc114" :answer/id #uuid "a4482997-246f-5013-aaa1-f55168fbc114" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e218adf9-787a-5fa5-9fc5-f2f5a38f2395" :answer/id #uuid "e218adf9-787a-5fa5-9fc5-f2f5a38f2395" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162599 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186160886 :answer-field/correct "answer-c3e7f79b-dd9d-5656-bdf4-163be92b98a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf79b104-5b48-5013-9b3f-1d9e6eb9c9b4" "answer-9a7de189-1716-5186-86d1-f361c08a7d77" "answer-fe4185ef-0279-5e7a-b0f9-ac161094b01d" "answer-6c406718-dcb5-57e1-82f4-30ae52dbf5ee" "answer-c3e7f79b-dd9d-5656-bdf4-163be92b98a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf79b104-5b48-5013-9b3f-1d9e6eb9c9b4" :answer/id #uuid "cf79b104-5b48-5013-9b3f-1d9e6eb9c9b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a7de189-1716-5186-86d1-f361c08a7d77" :answer/id #uuid "9a7de189-1716-5186-86d1-f361c08a7d77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe4185ef-0279-5e7a-b0f9-ac161094b01d" :answer/id #uuid "fe4185ef-0279-5e7a-b0f9-ac161094b01d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c406718-dcb5-57e1-82f4-30ae52dbf5ee" :answer/id #uuid "6c406718-dcb5-57e1-82f4-30ae52dbf5ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3e7f79b-dd9d-5656-bdf4-163be92b98a6" :answer/id #uuid "c3e7f79b-dd9d-5656-bdf4-163be92b98a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5d11f87e-0592-5806-9319-d12902b8a14a" :question/id #uuid "5d11f87e-0592-5806-9319-d12902b8a14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140837" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-020680cf-6e72-5c83-9a57-77d636592189"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-020680cf-6e72-5c83-9a57-77d636592189" :answer-field/id #uuid "020680cf-6e72-5c83-9a57-77d636592189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-33c29122-2095-5946-89a8-66b3a7b4a047" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33c29122-2095-5946-89a8-66b3a7b4a047" "answer-d48293d6-ba64-5760-b36c-7134921b5684" "answer-e1ce4336-3b77-5c48-831a-fa1b69e1b976" "answer-9fc11ff8-ed60-5bb7-b2f7-3dc537bc6067" "answer-0e504f2a-130f-56a5-8b73-6535b2866b21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33c29122-2095-5946-89a8-66b3a7b4a047" :answer/id #uuid "33c29122-2095-5946-89a8-66b3a7b4a047" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d48293d6-ba64-5760-b36c-7134921b5684" :answer/id #uuid "d48293d6-ba64-5760-b36c-7134921b5684" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1ce4336-3b77-5c48-831a-fa1b69e1b976" :answer/id #uuid "e1ce4336-3b77-5c48-831a-fa1b69e1b976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fc11ff8-ed60-5bb7-b2f7-3dc537bc6067" :answer/id #uuid "9fc11ff8-ed60-5bb7-b2f7-3dc537bc6067" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e504f2a-130f-56a5-8b73-6535b2866b21" :answer/id #uuid "0e504f2a-130f-56a5-8b73-6535b2866b21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d9482400-abcf-59ec-ba2d-fb58a274b62b" :question/id #uuid "d9482400-abcf-59ec-ba2d-fb58a274b62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94297" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dc95ee91-4a2e-557d-87e2-692ae59772de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dc95ee91-4a2e-557d-87e2-692ae59772de" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc95ee91-4a2e-557d-87e2-692ae59772de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f81e2cb1-6362-54d0-aabf-decb5246473d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78197d70-00a7-5346-922d-ec6723bfe518" "answer-f81e2cb1-6362-54d0-aabf-decb5246473d" "answer-b44f0b5f-d418-56ed-b60f-63dd89b63865" "answer-e8cb6331-4637-5828-97e4-26ba566dbbee" "answer-ee276881-acfd-54fc-ade7-b03deecf2e2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78197d70-00a7-5346-922d-ec6723bfe518" :answer/id #uuid "78197d70-00a7-5346-922d-ec6723bfe518" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f81e2cb1-6362-54d0-aabf-decb5246473d" :answer/id #uuid "f81e2cb1-6362-54d0-aabf-decb5246473d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b44f0b5f-d418-56ed-b60f-63dd89b63865" :answer/id #uuid "b44f0b5f-d418-56ed-b60f-63dd89b63865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8cb6331-4637-5828-97e4-26ba566dbbee" :answer/id #uuid "e8cb6331-4637-5828-97e4-26ba566dbbee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee276881-acfd-54fc-ade7-b03deecf2e2e" :answer/id #uuid "ee276881-acfd-54fc-ade7-b03deecf2e2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e34d3f6-344a-52de-9e80-5526338f9855" :question/id #uuid "9e34d3f6-344a-52de-9e80-5526338f9855" :question/math-academy-id "q-140843" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second and third columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the second and third columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fb1325f4-cc8e-5bd1-b83f-e768c4904fe9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb1325f4-cc8e-5bd1-b83f-e768c4904fe9" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1325f4-cc8e-5bd1-b83f-e768c4904fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b944de21-d177-5515-a01c-26253d331d73" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9a7e4db-fff0-5a80-b81b-a973c61d3d67" "answer-fa0732c4-d78b-5f63-bac2-1d79627fdaad" "answer-620276b0-0615-5b71-99fd-2a1e95b3a1c0" "answer-b944de21-d177-5515-a01c-26253d331d73" "answer-b249161d-1326-5a95-ab1f-9495d45cfa68"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9a7e4db-fff0-5a80-b81b-a973c61d3d67" :answer/id #uuid "a9a7e4db-fff0-5a80-b81b-a973c61d3d67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa0732c4-d78b-5f63-bac2-1d79627fdaad" :answer/id #uuid "fa0732c4-d78b-5f63-bac2-1d79627fdaad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-620276b0-0615-5b71-99fd-2a1e95b3a1c0" :answer/id #uuid "620276b0-0615-5b71-99fd-2a1e95b3a1c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b944de21-d177-5515-a01c-26253d331d73" :answer/id #uuid "b944de21-d177-5515-a01c-26253d331d73" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b249161d-1326-5a95-ab1f-9495d45cfa68" :answer/id #uuid "b249161d-1326-5a95-ab1f-9495d45cfa68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f400860-7ffc-5147-852c-7cca9efa6b2f" :question/id #uuid "2f400860-7ffc-5147-852c-7cca9efa6b2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94346" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=7.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1deb190f-aaea-51df-a506-e53c0439123b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1deb190f-aaea-51df-a506-e53c0439123b" :answer-field/id #uuid "1deb190f-aaea-51df-a506-e53c0439123b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9d55135c-e6fc-540d-b590-5fdddcd8980b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5691b04-e00a-5077-8a2d-e2eee3328fd5" "answer-9d55135c-e6fc-540d-b590-5fdddcd8980b" "answer-2284ef03-aeaa-5e50-9406-e44a27d2edd0" "answer-2fdbc765-9651-5639-8112-8a0f046be0ec" "answer-ad2b44c2-eb45-57f5-a1dc-bce8c3dfce15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5691b04-e00a-5077-8a2d-e2eee3328fd5" :answer/id #uuid "e5691b04-e00a-5077-8a2d-e2eee3328fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d55135c-e6fc-540d-b590-5fdddcd8980b" :answer/id #uuid "9d55135c-e6fc-540d-b590-5fdddcd8980b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2284ef03-aeaa-5e50-9406-e44a27d2edd0" :answer/id #uuid "2284ef03-aeaa-5e50-9406-e44a27d2edd0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fdbc765-9651-5639-8112-8a0f046be0ec" :answer/id #uuid "2fdbc765-9651-5639-8112-8a0f046be0ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad2b44c2-eb45-57f5-a1dc-bce8c3dfce15" :answer/id #uuid "ad2b44c2-eb45-57f5-a1dc-bce8c3dfce15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163223 :tutorial/content "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a real matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nThe procedure for finding an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3 \\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ is similar to diagonalizing a regular (diagonalizable) $3\\times 3$ matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nAs an example, consider the matrix \n![](images/ac/acba5373e2f2470930adec855df9958508d9f41aba5d3fc6988b0001c07a9dfd.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. So let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\qquad \\lambda_2=-6, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-9.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** We need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/cd/cddf44571b3aba21dca82cc12664733469c17f30ed3a9a9cfc62acb12230caef.svg)\nNotice that the eigenvectors are mutually orthogonal. \nNow, an orthogonal diagonalization requires each eigenvector to be normalized. So, dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the following unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/e4/e4bfc5b51cea64bd5cb8d340fbeab1a126aaa3f48ae42b991c2b5af2a25480b3.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/4b/4b4a8c98b7c4688fab231510b6b1d615f47bed070310d39f3e812cd9fb811395.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** In this example, we automatically get orthogonal eigenvectors because $A$ has three *distinct* eigenvalues. However, in the case of repeated eigenvalues, the corresponding eigenvectors might not be orthogonal." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163249 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/28/2807ddab01a673d01c385b0dcc1b77d007896f803376006e7f81f639930fecdc.svg)\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n![](images/95/95d0808a376ad679118ee26b9387cc3037862b74f12945db886a2ffd7738d525.svg)\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n![](images/b4/b4aaec2c201e4ae0aff1054c57fdf4cc860dac1fa6bd38a9b58865b5306b8ce2.svg)\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n![](images/c9/c9a112d21b6868b583f521a73a5aa491a40ce46c365adf6ddc3f7eeb60c9f5fe.svg)\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/f1/f111684678ff06708513e20f22db25b4d6246dda2a8ce5f50b5905c9a13b0cbd.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162215 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073876 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4149fc67-1e41-5028-8a46-16f732885a43" "question-74744733-4a48-54d6-9cdb-1913c4d1cafb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073584 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents a perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}?$\n\n![](images/90/90e49cf78c7fb86b6f5a8c33b3e5bd28279248f791757a2351ddb86296d79d72.png)" :question/worked-solution "A perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$:\n\n- must split $\\overline{AB}$ in half, and\n\n- must be perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nAmong the given options, only diagram IV shows a perpendicular bisector to the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\nNotice that in diagrams I, II and III we have bisectors but they are not perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073877 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-53541935-a208-5c44-81fe-b4833a040db0" "question-73bc99c1-88cb-54fb-b573-fbe1a7f6c64f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073585 :question/problem "Solve for $x$ in the figure below given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\n![](images/2d/2dea948c7ab1cf3bb7f543c651d98f23cda8531bbec8cc44e12736c3f04f04c3.png)" :question/worked-solution "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DA & =DB \\\\ x+5 & =2x \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073878 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d96f4929-b558-5dc7-a6d9-bc9fa1be46fa" "question-0505a08a-9b50-54e3-aef5-883b15a54631" "question-30d08ba8-2476-53e5-a489-f0d0e93d5c87"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073586 :question/problem "Solve for $x$ in the figure below given that $AD=BD$ and $AE=BE.$ \n\n![](images/e5/e58ea5aadf9075a8b1d7a3de3a3c82e88eb3b47281cf75134631d3a153d42127.png)" :question/worked-solution "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $D$ and $E$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}.$ Therefore, $D$ and $E$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $AC=BC.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =BC \\\\ 3x+2 & =5x-2 \\\\ 4 & =2x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4149fc67-1e41-5028-8a46-16f732885a43" :question/id #uuid "4149fc67-1e41-5028-8a46-16f732885a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-53044" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd7bf8806014c80bbe0341c6f3091584cd04c6fc846fb0534119f6ccdf32b102.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above represents a bisector (perpendicular or not perpendicular) of $\\overset{―}{KL}?$" :question/worked-solution "A bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}$:\n\n- must split $\\overset{―}{KL}$ in half.\n\nAmong the given options, only diagrams II and IV show a bisector to the line segment $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nNotice that in diagrams I and III the intersection of the two segments does not split $\\overset{―}{KL}$ in half." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-35c4bc8c-2993-5baa-8f3b-3d3d52b82da8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35c4bc8c-2993-5baa-8f3b-3d3d52b82da8" :answer-field/id #uuid "35c4bc8c-2993-5baa-8f3b-3d3d52b82da8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f6e264ff-c2c2-53af-85e9-f0c8bbeb24df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3822e7ac-d037-5f81-979a-e8b10abbb077" "answer-27fd5638-4f51-5e3c-a384-36d4371da7ce" "answer-f6e26