eld/correct "answer-2abeecee-defe-5aba-af93-f4e6edf7e994" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14e399eb-c610-5a70-9be3-c5cf8318d749" "answer-2abeecee-defe-5aba-af93-f4e6edf7e994" "answer-2e8c691c-01ff-5adf-a9c8-3026fd1cc52f" "answer-51556f70-c451-5644-835a-916d2319bd5f" "answer-b140c6dc-b0fd-525e-9a4c-62a9f172f1fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14e399eb-c610-5a70-9be3-c5cf8318d749" :answer/id #uuid "14e399eb-c610-5a70-9be3-c5cf8318d749" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2abeecee-defe-5aba-af93-f4e6edf7e994" :answer/id #uuid "2abeecee-defe-5aba-af93-f4e6edf7e994" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e8c691c-01ff-5adf-a9c8-3026fd1cc52f" :answer/id #uuid "2e8c691c-01ff-5adf-a9c8-3026fd1cc52f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51556f70-c451-5644-835a-916d2319bd5f" :answer/id #uuid "51556f70-c451-5644-835a-916d2319bd5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b140c6dc-b0fd-525e-9a4c-62a9f172f1fd" :answer/id #uuid "b140c6dc-b0fd-525e-9a4c-62a9f172f1fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-30d08ba8-2476-53e5-a489-f0d0e93d5c87" :question/id #uuid "30d08ba8-2476-53e5-a489-f0d0e93d5c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-67780" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b89ec998b55eacb8e5de3bf0c22aa94d351232f38459c17640956f96e939bac.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $KN=LN$ and $KP=LP.$" :question/worked-solution "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $P$ and $N$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ Therefore, $P$ and $N$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{PN}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $KM=LM.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}KM & =LM \\\\ 8x-6 & =7x+1 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-275786d1-e16f-57d5-90f0-1ca2711b1c9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-275786d1-e16f-57d5-90f0-1ca2711b1c9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "275786d1-e16f-57d5-90f0-1ca2711b1c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a093d82-aec6-51b8-919d-9c3fb850f1cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15968d7e-2f5a-5796-a92d-eab48c29c4d9" "answer-12c8a65e-a963-526f-8695-e1d98de36624" "answer-3a093d82-aec6-51b8-919d-9c3fb850f1cd" "answer-8eb599f7-5f9a-59f1-af2b-ac437bac25af" "answer-5d0c2cf8-b521-5f0e-a3a9-e7f20602123c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15968d7e-2f5a-5796-a92d-eab48c29c4d9" :answer/id #uuid "15968d7e-2f5a-5796-a92d-eab48c29c4d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12c8a65e-a963-526f-8695-e1d98de36624" :answer/id #uuid "12c8a65e-a963-526f-8695-e1d98de36624" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a093d82-aec6-51b8-919d-9c3fb850f1cd" :answer/id #uuid "3a093d82-aec6-51b8-919d-9c3fb850f1cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8eb599f7-5f9a-59f1-af2b-ac437bac25af" :answer/id #uuid "8eb599f7-5f9a-59f1-af2b-ac437bac25af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d0c2cf8-b521-5f0e-a3a9-e7f20602123c" :answer/id #uuid "5d0c2cf8-b521-5f0e-a3a9-e7f20602123c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074976 :tutorial/content "Two intersecting lines are **perpendicular** if they form a right angle (an angle that measures $90^\\circ$). \n\n![](images/ee/ee6258a8a148ae3a8f4ce63321bf9f4dfa955eb0a90b332477e6cc933cad7b74.png)\n\nFor example, the two lines above are perpendicular because the angle $\\angle BOD$ is a right angle.\n\nThe opposite is also true: if two lines are perpendicular, then they meet at a right angle.\n\nAs you may have noticed already, when two lines are perpendicular, they don't form only one right angle. All the four angles formed by the intersection are right angles!\n\n![](images/15/152aa56c4fd669e417cb20f1bef8fcec7e7ea34e0be9e44e7acad3ac490e5713.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075003 :tutorial/content "A segment **bisector** is any segment, line, or ray that splits another segment into two congruent parts.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is a bisector of the segment $\\overline{ST}.$\n\n![](images/1c/1cf8d2956dd054509cb421a1b706063ea318f14d0575295634baf0fc5679105c.png)\n\nA **perpendicular bisector** is any segment, line, or ray that bisects another segment *and is perpendicular to that segment*.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{UV}$ is a perpendicular bisector of the segment $\\overline{ST}.$\n\n![](images/01/01cf976113ad5c2f635548aab38434c4add38c61195aacc96e611077aa4df606.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074987 :tutorial/content "The **perpendicular bisector  theorem** states that:\n\nIn a plane, if a point is on the perpendicular bisector of a segment, then it is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, we see that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n![](images/b4/b45782d21e6ffdff1fd46956b1933d976df90e69f8d5e15e2edf116fe11c0e8e.png)\n\n Consequently, we can conclude that the point $D$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}.$ So we have $AD=BD.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074988 :tutorial/content "The **converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem** states that:\n\nIn a plane, if a point is equidistant from the endpoints of a segment, then it is on the perpendicular bisector of the segment.\n\nFor example, in the diagram below, we see that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is perpendicular to $\\overline{AB},$ and the point $D$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\n![](images/d1/d17f25984a6e153a6f155552e7da4a0d67241ad7e83eabefb5ad5b663827fbce.png)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$ In particular, we conclude that $AC = BC.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075002 :tutorial/content "The most important property of perpendicular lines is the following.\n\nGiven a line $l$ and a point $P$, there is one and only one line $m$ that passes through $P$ and is perpendicular to $l$.\n\n![](images/24/24e78b2f90aa4cfd9a6eba5ace46195536377c19d255e51e82485af69c866292.png)\n\nOther nice properties include the following:\n\n- If two distinct lines are perpendicular to the same (third) line, then these two lines are parallel. For example, in the figure below, both lines $n$ and $m$ are perpendicular to the line $l$. As a result, $n \\parallel m$.\n\n- If a line is perpendicular to one of two parallel lines, then that line is also perpendicular to the second parallel line.\n\n![](images/64/64bae80f45df4e2536e3c929471352e9d911607efeeab9bd835acebf4ff0af01.png)\n\nSymbol $\\perp$\n\nFinally, if a line $p$ is perpendicular to a line $q$, we can denote it without words using the symbol $\\perp$ (perpendicular), as follows:\n\n$$\np \\perp q\n$$\n\nThis reads as \"*$p$ is perpendicular to $q$*.\"" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073943 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186173866 :answer-field/choices 17592186173867] [:db/retract 17592186173866 :answer-field/choices 17592186173868] [:db/retract 17592186173866 :answer-field/choices 17592186173869] [:db/retract 17592186173866 :answer-field/choices 17592186173870] [:db/retract 17592186173866 :answer-field/choices 17592186173871] {:db/id 17592186176087 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0592700d-3dcd-547f-b289-799124f15c20"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175719 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of these transformations map $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto a line?\n\n![](images/89/8914e3b11b6ccb726df2363fbc238d4c87ab6948e217ea54617e574a4babc4f2.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176088 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0ea7759f-f796-5f54-9f1a-8b557e8042a4" "question-18ff2792-817e-5c06-978e-de39e8bf5d7d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175720 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n![](images/bf/bfab47037972e5cbb408473b3c94463b8100d622971ed66ba5dabc9ff15be7bb.svg)\nFind the equation of the line that the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63cc3457-67ea-5db0-a46f-35ae7d0b8b95"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175721 :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=-5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\langle -1, 5 \\rangle \\in \\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$\n\nII. $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\Bbb R^2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0592700d-3dcd-547f-b289-799124f15c20" :question/id #uuid "0592700d-3dcd-547f-b289-799124f15c20" :question/math-academy-id "q-111770" :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto the whole of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear transformation over ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- ${T}_{1}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ since ${T}_{1}$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{1})=0.$\n\n- ${T}_{2}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of ${T}_{2}$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}({T}_{2})=1.$\n\n- ${T}_{3}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Indeed, ${T}_{3}$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{3})=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{3}$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c93982c4-dd30-5e4e-a9ea-b96ef2930787"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c93982c4-dd30-5e4e-a9ea-b96ef2930787" :answer-field/id #uuid "c93982c4-dd30-5e4e-a9ea-b96ef2930787" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-36b9266c-9397-5034-989f-335e8a9fb05e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a3d7777-5f4f-5586-8fa8-35c43f3d0ca6" "answer-3385bc8c-13a8-5ac9-b762-a9c4ef66f0ee" "answer-65225491-2eeb-526a-a29a-3e7df44c64de" "answer-a1ea921a-f5c0-55a7-bb26-4d4212583925" "answer-36b9266c-9397-5034-989f-335e8a9fb05e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a3d7777-5f4f-5586-8fa8-35c43f3d0ca6" :answer/id #uuid "6a3d7777-5f4f-5586-8fa8-35c43f3d0ca6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3385bc8c-13a8-5ac9-b762-a9c4ef66f0ee" :answer/id #uuid "3385bc8c-13a8-5ac9-b762-a9c4ef66f0ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65225491-2eeb-526a-a29a-3e7df44c64de" :answer/id #uuid "65225491-2eeb-526a-a29a-3e7df44c64de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1ea921a-f5c0-55a7-bb26-4d4212583925" :answer/id #uuid "a1ea921a-f5c0-55a7-bb26-4d4212583925" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36b9266c-9397-5034-989f-335e8a9fb05e" :answer/id #uuid "36b9266c-9397-5034-989f-335e8a9fb05e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176268 :question/problem "Consider the standard matrices of three linear transformations shown below. Which of the transformations map ${R}^{2}$ onto the whole of ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\n{T}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{T}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear transformation over ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T,$ we have the following:\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ i.e., $T$ is nonsingular.\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\n- $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ if and only if $\\text{rank}(T)=0,$ i.e., $T$ is the zero matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- ${T}_{1}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto ${R}^{2}.$ Indeed, ${T}_{1}$ is a nonsingular matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{1})=2.$\n\n- ${T}_{2}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto $(0,0)$ since ${T}_{2}$ is the zero matrix, i.e. $\\text{rank}({T}_{2})=0.$\n\n- ${T}_{3}$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line. Notice that the columns of ${T}_{3}$ are linearly dependent but it's not the zero matrix. So, $\\text{rank}({T}_{3})=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173866 :answer-field/correct "answer-adb92ef0-4bc8-5da6-b999-7ea14ed46612" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf069599-41bb-5a02-9dd2-d0f9f1d1f506" "answer-adb92ef0-4bc8-5da6-b999-7ea14ed46612" "answer-50535676-b665-5456-ac4b-92d32aaa1648" "answer-0f6412b2-f3f4-5c23-b342-866bfbb9e350" "answer-2a25603c-117e-5c80-8a51-d915907e44b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf069599-41bb-5a02-9dd2-d0f9f1d1f506" :answer/id #uuid "bf069599-41bb-5a02-9dd2-d0f9f1d1f506" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adb92ef0-4bc8-5da6-b999-7ea14ed46612" :answer/id #uuid "adb92ef0-4bc8-5da6-b999-7ea14ed46612" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{1}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50535676-b665-5456-ac4b-92d32aaa1648" :answer/id #uuid "50535676-b665-5456-ac4b-92d32aaa1648" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f6412b2-f3f4-5c23-b342-866bfbb9e350" :answer/id #uuid "0f6412b2-f3f4-5c23-b342-866bfbb9e350" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a25603c-117e-5c80-8a51-d915907e44b4" :answer/id #uuid "2a25603c-117e-5c80-8a51-d915907e44b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${T}_{3}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0ea7759f-f796-5f54-9f1a-8b557e8042a4" :question/id #uuid "0ea7759f-f796-5f54-9f1a-8b557e8042a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-111788" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(1,3),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{3-0}{1-0}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=3x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe0672c6-a8a1-54ee-b67e-0f22d1bf3419"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe0672c6-a8a1-54ee-b67e-0f22d1bf3419" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0672c6-a8a1-54ee-b67e-0f22d1bf3419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a8cca849-8dd3-5d33-8394-2e0d4cedf5ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88430c53-2ff5-5c57-bc6b-a66b7136b41c" "answer-a8cca849-8dd3-5d33-8394-2e0d4cedf5ad" "answer-4ca6c8b6-2f80-5c70-9069-f0586e112d1a" "answer-2b324769-9430-5f5c-80c0-9c13d075c858" "answer-a0602523-5644-52d8-bb00-13c7914d03e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88430c53-2ff5-5c57-bc6b-a66b7136b41c" :answer/id #uuid "88430c53-2ff5-5c57-bc6b-a66b7136b41c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8cca849-8dd3-5d33-8394-2e0d4cedf5ad" :answer/id #uuid "a8cca849-8dd3-5d33-8394-2e0d4cedf5ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ca6c8b6-2f80-5c70-9069-f0586e112d1a" :answer/id #uuid "4ca6c8b6-2f80-5c70-9069-f0586e112d1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b324769-9430-5f5c-80c0-9c13d075c858" :answer/id #uuid "2b324769-9430-5f5c-80c0-9c13d075c858" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0602523-5644-52d8-bb00-13c7914d03e9" :answer/id #uuid "a0602523-5644-52d8-bb00-13c7914d03e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-18ff2792-817e-5c06-978e-de39e8bf5d7d" :question/id #uuid "18ff2792-817e-5c06-978e-de39e8bf5d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97072" :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the points $(1,2)$ and $(4,8),$ which are defined by the columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{8-2}{4-1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=2x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ca172406-8acb-556c-ac95-a056ce4c8607"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ca172406-8acb-556c-ac95-a056ce4c8607" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca172406-8acb-556c-ac95-a056ce4c8607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2c01bddf-505a-542a-9412-fb0c27435749" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c46cf5d3-873d-529b-8f60-aafbf3020809" "answer-2c01bddf-505a-542a-9412-fb0c27435749" "answer-9f8e000b-f0fd-595a-9369-252eec48837d" "answer-47eebad0-cd40-5fde-9742-d083f03326fc" "answer-40b8ac61-afc4-560b-8da5-9b5b28a20dc4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c46cf5d3-873d-529b-8f60-aafbf3020809" :answer/id #uuid "c46cf5d3-873d-529b-8f60-aafbf3020809" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c01bddf-505a-542a-9412-fb0c27435749" :answer/id #uuid "2c01bddf-505a-542a-9412-fb0c27435749" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f8e000b-f0fd-595a-9369-252eec48837d" :answer/id #uuid "9f8e000b-f0fd-595a-9369-252eec48837d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47eebad0-cd40-5fde-9742-d083f03326fc" :answer/id #uuid "47eebad0-cd40-5fde-9742-d083f03326fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40b8ac61-afc4-560b-8da5-9b5b28a20dc4" :answer/id #uuid "40b8ac61-afc4-560b-8da5-9b5b28a20dc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-63cc3457-67ea-5db0-a46f-35ae7d0b8b95" :question/id #uuid "63cc3457-67ea-5db0-a46f-35ae7d0b8b95" :question/math-academy-id "q-97076" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n\nII. $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Im}(T)$ contains no vectors of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace $\\{0\\},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The image of $T$ contains the zero vector $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a506a0a-a4f1-5314-a81c-bab95d6f2544"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a506a0a-a4f1-5314-a81c-bab95d6f2544" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a506a0a-a4f1-5314-a81c-bab95d6f2544" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9c68103e-c97a-5610-b9f4-d1051647b15f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7da15e7c-1166-51c6-897a-9f8a3708487a" "answer-ef414556-5a7d-50d4-9453-5c30d34e1bcd" "answer-10db60f2-620a-5b8c-8ce4-8400f4bceb9a" "answer-9c68103e-c97a-5610-b9f4-d1051647b15f" "answer-cd93118a-b15b-5093-91cb-5221765965a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7da15e7c-1166-51c6-897a-9f8a3708487a" :answer/id #uuid "7da15e7c-1166-51c6-897a-9f8a3708487a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef414556-5a7d-50d4-9453-5c30d34e1bcd" :answer/id #uuid "ef414556-5a7d-50d4-9453-5c30d34e1bcd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10db60f2-620a-5b8c-8ce4-8400f4bceb9a" :answer/id #uuid "10db60f2-620a-5b8c-8ce4-8400f4bceb9a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c68103e-c97a-5610-b9f4-d1051647b15f" :answer/id #uuid "9c68103e-c97a-5610-b9f4-d1051647b15f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd93118a-b15b-5093-91cb-5221765965a6" :answer/id #uuid "cd93118a-b15b-5093-91cb-5221765965a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177088 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following linear transformation: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}^2\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Therefore, it can only be one of the following:  \n\n- the whole of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$\n\n- a line through the origin, or\n\n- a single point (vector), namely the origin $(0,0).$\n\nLet's denote the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ by $T.$ It turns out that the dimension of the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ depends on $\\text{rank}(T)$ (or equivalently on $\\text{nullity}(T)$ by the rank-nullity theorem). Indeed, we have the following:\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=2,$ then the two columns of $T$ are linearly independent. This means $T$ is invertible and therefore, $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) =  \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto  $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7d/7de193042587cb9362641cbb541477a5e4951e96d9e82f074fb0cc3a86a80d3a.svg)\n\n![](images/88/88baa47cae14d4a43d59b366c13b3c29376678edef2fe34ff505a942ef6af986.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=1,$ then there is a nonzero vector that generates the image of $\\mathbf{T}.$ In this case, the image of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a one-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^2$. So, it is a line passing through the origin.\n\nFor example, the following transformation maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto the line $y=2x\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/49/4952615b0c4ddf85cc6d60483f8c0587093c67734a320cde913644fe8fdafb55.svg)\nNotice that the points $(2,4)$ and $(1,2),$ defined by the columns of $T,$ lie on the line $y=2x.$\n\n![](images/03/0322f183c16fe82515277d10cf27a93ae753f8eadf7eeb1db4d3c5e404480b09.png)\n\n- If $\\textrm{rank}(T)=0$ then,  according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that  $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})=\\mathbf{0}$ for all $\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Bbb R^2$. So, in this case, we have that\n![](images/4a/4a0ce67cffc02e28edffb9efeedca2e21ffc7c0d8b72ccfa0566cb29f6679865.svg)\nwhich maps $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto $\\{(0,0)\\},$ i.e., the set containing only the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/12/1252907a1e9767201dd326945f328f91fc493ea283b244dbfee42a7c4248d9e6.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176160 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-33d92120-f62e-56e8-8525-dfbf23370577"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092559 :question/problem "A math teacher splits her calculus class into three groups such that $35%$ of all the students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C. If $20%$ of the students in Group A obtained the top grade, $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, and the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade?" :question/worked-solution "Let $T$ be the event that a chosen student obtained the top grade, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group A,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the student is from Group B,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the student is from Group С.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T\\cap A)+P(T\\cap B)+P(T\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P(B)=0.35,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group A, and $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.2,\\,P(T|B)=0.1\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.255.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(T|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.255 & =(0.2)(0.35)+(0.1)(0.35)+P(T|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.255 & =0.07+0.035+0.3P(T|C) \\\\ 0.3P(T|C) & =0.15 \\\\ P(T|C) & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade is $0.50.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186089525 :answer-field/correct 17592186089530 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092314 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e343068f-45c7-52dd-ac5c-c29d4a7a5cbc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e343068f-45c7-52dd-ac5c-c29d4a7a5cbc" :question/id #uuid "e343068f-45c7-52dd-ac5c-c29d4a7a5cbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-143559" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. The components are produced by three machines, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ In a particular random sample, $31$ of the components were produced by machine $A,$ $34$ by machine $B,$ and $35$ by machine $C.$ On inspecting the sample for defective components, it was found that $2$ defective components were produced by machine $A,$ $1$  by machine $B,$ and $2$  by machine $C.$ Based on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the  component was produced  by  machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component from the sample is defective, we use the law of total probability:  \n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap C)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that there is a total of \n\n$$\n31+34+35=100\n$$\n\ncomponents in the sample.\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap A),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $A$ $(2)$ and divide by the total number of components produced $(100).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(S\\cap A)=\\frac{2}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap B),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $B$ $(1)$ and divide by the total number of components produced  $(100).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(S\\cap B)=\\frac{1}{100}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(S\\cap C),$ we take the number defective components produced by machine $C$ $(2)$ and divide by the total number of components produced  $(100).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(S\\cap C)=\\frac{2}{100}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap A)+P(S\\cap B)+P(S\\cap C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{100}+\\frac{1}{100}+\\frac{2}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea73b3dd-bf88-5f7b-9b97-f1ed24555540"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea73b3dd-bf88-5f7b-9b97-f1ed24555540" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea73b3dd-bf88-5f7b-9b97-f1ed24555540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0145d796-eb28-54a8-95f3-28e7aefa0081" :answer-field/choices ["answer-693adf90-92ac-557a-8ea3-07804f12f595" "answer-0145d796-eb28-54a8-95f3-28e7aefa0081" "answer-78ae7c7a-fba7-5249-b0f2-20cf7941b79c" "answer-b321f881-cce5-5d65-bf3b-f929adf85f17" "answer-45356b43-9eb8-5668-952a-155b42bb6eb7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-693adf90-92ac-557a-8ea3-07804f12f595" :answer/id #uuid "693adf90-92ac-557a-8ea3-07804f12f595" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0145d796-eb28-54a8-95f3-28e7aefa0081" :answer/id #uuid "0145d796-eb28-54a8-95f3-28e7aefa0081" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78ae7c7a-fba7-5249-b0f2-20cf7941b79c" :answer/id #uuid "78ae7c7a-fba7-5249-b0f2-20cf7941b79c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b321f881-cce5-5d65-bf3b-f929adf85f17" :answer/id #uuid "b321f881-cce5-5d65-bf3b-f929adf85f17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{35}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45356b43-9eb8-5668-952a-155b42bb6eb7" :answer/id #uuid "45356b43-9eb8-5668-952a-155b42bb6eb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a98874b4-d2c1-50c9-83d1-a1b2a1981db4" "question-aa34c10c-c85d-5965-879f-040a2dcc2a58"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a98874b4-d2c1-50c9-83d1-a1b2a1981db4" :question/id #uuid "a98874b4-d2c1-50c9-83d1-a1b2a1981db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-143721" :question/problem "Three factories produce the same tool for the market. Factory $A$ produces $30%$ of the tools, of which $2%$ are defective. Factory $B$ produces $34%$ of the tools, of which $5%$ are defective, and factory $C$ produces $36%$ of the tools, of which $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected tool is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the tool was produced by factory $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since factory $A$ produces $30%$ of the tools, factory $B$ produces $34%$ of the tools, and factory $C$ produces $36%$ of the tools, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.34,\\,P(C)=0.36.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of the tools produced by factory $A$, $5%$ of the tools produced by factory $B$, and $5%$ of the tools produced by factory $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.02,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected tool is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.02)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.34)+(0.05)(0.36) \\\\  & =0.006+0.017+0.018 \\\\  & =0.041.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-03277a1a-6eea-5e2a-9857-810f8df632af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03277a1a-6eea-5e2a-9857-810f8df632af" :answer-field/id #uuid "03277a1a-6eea-5e2a-9857-810f8df632af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5282849f-2a3c-5f32-b6be-060b8370b7f4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24a9eb73-0014-5463-b63e-0a940d9e2582" "answer-5282849f-2a3c-5f32-b6be-060b8370b7f4" "answer-f8c243cf-613b-5eb8-9ed8-a7b18e1d6ddb" "answer-7d996b1c-82f7-598f-be8d-1b498be533e4" "answer-5460928c-e102-597f-9db9-80c61e0a7e02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24a9eb73-0014-5463-b63e-0a940d9e2582" :answer/id #uuid "24a9eb73-0014-5463-b63e-0a940d9e2582" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.036" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5282849f-2a3c-5f32-b6be-060b8370b7f4" :answer/id #uuid "5282849f-2a3c-5f32-b6be-060b8370b7f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.041" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8c243cf-613b-5eb8-9ed8-a7b18e1d6ddb" :answer/id #uuid "f8c243cf-613b-5eb8-9ed8-a7b18e1d6ddb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.047" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d996b1c-82f7-598f-be8d-1b498be533e4" :answer/id #uuid "7d996b1c-82f7-598f-be8d-1b498be533e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.049" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5460928c-e102-597f-9db9-80c61e0a7e02" :answer/id #uuid "5460928c-e102-597f-9db9-80c61e0a7e02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.052" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa34c10c-c85d-5965-879f-040a2dcc2a58" :question/id #uuid "aa34c10c-c85d-5965-879f-040a2dcc2a58" :question/math-academy-id "q-143759" :question/problem "Ann has three email accounts. In the first account, she receives $40%$ of her emails, of which $10%$ are spam. In the second account, she receives $35%$ of her emails, of which $8%$ are spam. And in the third account, she receives $25%$ of her emails, of which $8%$ are spam. Based on this data, what is the probability that a randomly selected email from any of Ann's accounts is spam?" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be the event that the selected email is spam, and\n\n- let ${A}_{1}$ be the event that the email was received in the first account,\n\n- let ${A}_{2}$ be the event that the email was received in the second account,\n\n- let ${A}_{3}$ be the event that the email was received in the third account.\n\nThen the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S\\cap {A}_{1})+P(S\\cap {A}_{2})+P(S\\cap {A}_{3}) \\\\  & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since Ann receives $40%$ of her emails in the first account,  $35%$ in the second account, and $25%$ in the third account, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})=0.4,\\,P({A}_{2})=0.35,\\,P({A}_{3})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of the emails received in the first account, $8%$ of the emails received in the first account, and $8%$ of the emails received in the third account are spam, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|{A}_{1})=0.1,\\,P(S|{A}_{2})=0.08\\,P(S|{A}_{3})=0.08.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected email from any of Ann's accounts is spam is   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(S|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(S|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3}) \\\\  & =(0.1)(0.4)+(0.08)(0.35)+(0.08)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.04+0.028+0.02 \\\\  & =0.088.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e0a0ca37-d77c-51fb-b507-a74b429aeacd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e0a0ca37-d77c-51fb-b507-a74b429aeacd" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a0ca37-d77c-51fb-b507-a74b429aeacd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5fd79dd8-46de-5029-9f80-7bb504d1b873" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed962bd1-71d7-545c-8b5c-392d81210bc2" "answer-5b399609-7f79-5173-9ee0-8e611fd769c7" "answer-837910a1-2e2b-5bf0-9265-dbc58e68c246" "answer-5fd79dd8-46de-5029-9f80-7bb504d1b873" "answer-e355882d-ac3b-5bff-9a6a-e73a0f92e528"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed962bd1-71d7-545c-8b5c-392d81210bc2" :answer/id #uuid "ed962bd1-71d7-545c-8b5c-392d81210bc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.062" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b399609-7f79-5173-9ee0-8e611fd769c7" :answer/id #uuid "5b399609-7f79-5173-9ee0-8e611fd769c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.068" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-837910a1-2e2b-5bf0-9265-dbc58e68c246" :answer/id #uuid "837910a1-2e2b-5bf0-9265-dbc58e68c246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.072" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fd79dd8-46de-5029-9f80-7bb504d1b873" :answer/id #uuid "5fd79dd8-46de-5029-9f80-7bb504d1b873" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.088" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e355882d-ac3b-5bff-9a6a-e73a0f92e528" :answer/id #uuid "e355882d-ac3b-5bff-9a6a-e73a0f92e528" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.092" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-33d92120-f62e-56e8-8525-dfbf23370577" :question/id #uuid "33d92120-f62e-56e8-8525-dfbf23370577" :question/math-academy-id "q-143978" :question/problem "In a hardware store, there are three boxes containing light bulbs. Box $A$ contains $15$ bulbs, of which $2$ are defective, and box $B$ contains $10$ bulbs, of which $1$ is defective. A box is selected at random, and a bulb is randomly chosen from the box. If the probability that the selected bulb is defective is $\\frac{2}{15},$ what is the probability that a randomly selected bulb from box $C$ is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, and   \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is  from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since a box is chosen at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A$ and  $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$  we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected bulb is defective is $\\frac{2}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =(\\frac{2}{15})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(D|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ P(D|C) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected bulb from box $C$ is defective is $\\frac{1}{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c481d0c-6bec-5a2b-817b-51131184fbf0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c481d0c-6bec-5a2b-817b-51131184fbf0" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c481d0c-6bec-5a2b-817b-51131184fbf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8aefa18a-70e2-59eb-a0e8-c4294cece2a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffe75c90-a8f8-5625-9747-315993606532" "answer-1443b175-b2e6-58e7-9266-46bb002bf1d9" "answer-8aefa18a-70e2-59eb-a0e8-c4294cece2a2" "answer-731bab39-0cc7-53b5-8a98-4114c8993795" "answer-92f9cac9-b592-5ea1-b5f0-997e20fedd02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffe75c90-a8f8-5625-9747-315993606532" :answer/id #uuid "ffe75c90-a8f8-5625-9747-315993606532" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1443b175-b2e6-58e7-9266-46bb002bf1d9" :answer/id #uuid "1443b175-b2e6-58e7-9266-46bb002bf1d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8aefa18a-70e2-59eb-a0e8-c4294cece2a2" :answer/id #uuid "8aefa18a-70e2-59eb-a0e8-c4294cece2a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-731bab39-0cc7-53b5-8a98-