p/id #uuid "a66ad3c1-21d4-525d-b81a-6c389b90b1cb" :step/content "question-33d92120-f62e-56e8-8525-dfbf23370577" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110547 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6cd0a1a3-7b23-5387-830e-7fe8d908d132" "question-1962d5a7-c8a1-5f9e-a8c4-8c270c23c07a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110436 :question/problem "Given the figure below, find the value of $x.$\n\n![](images/8b/8b89eec86675b2bebfda74ecf1da8e0b7980e5de6d3a52de84e93e64a4a62068.png)" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. \nSince vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{12}^{∘} & ={54}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={42}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={21}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110749 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-804e107f-83a7-522e-91c4-4c6cf836e13f" "question-19c18e1c-068f-53bc-9c01-193db0152518"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110383 :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{15}^{∘} & =2x+{10}^{∘} \\\\ x-{15}^{∘} & ={10}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={25}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=25^\\circ,$ we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $2x + 10^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n2x + 10^\\circ = 2 \\cdot 25^\\circ + 10^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of $60^\\circ.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110751 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-97e7e9d8-dcf7-50ae-a8f6-45c7e2519058" "question-5703c4ee-d713-54f9-901a-aaa46a569aaf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110384 :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\angle AOB$ is a straight angle. Determine $m \\angle AOD.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eac6c11dcc513cdf896cdfaa9650689af1bf58afd147783d0bb1ca61dd3a324.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle AOB$ is straight, $m\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ.$ So by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC + m\\angle BOC = 180^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+{100}^{∘})+11x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 16x+{100}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 16x & ={80}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=5^\\circ,$ we can calculate $m \\angle COB$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm \\angle COB = 11(5^\\circ) = 55^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $\\angle AOD$ and $\\angle COB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOD = m \\angle COB = 55^\\circ\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110753 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f79072b2-718e-5891-9f14-a1fea25a8bca" "question-b19a18fa-549e-563d-980d-10ad4fe9bdeb" "question-2571ba1e-66f6-5370-ac1a-d3c46be963c3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110385 :question/problem "In the diagram shown below, it is known that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ.$ Determine the value of $m \\angle 2 + m \\angle 4.$\n\n![](images/1b/1bc662904a52724fac28dc180f70088592ff4da181930c6607b1bb590b3ec188.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1=m∠2, \\\\ m∠3=m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{60}^{∘}=m∠2, \\\\ {120}^{∘}=m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠2+m∠4 & ={60}^{∘}+{120}^{∘} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6cd0a1a3-7b23-5387-830e-7fe8d908d132" :question/id #uuid "6cd0a1a3-7b23-5387-830e-7fe8d908d132" :question/math-academy-id "q-114804" :question/problem "![](images/50/5084bbe406dd4d44ffdeb6e485f6cf363780de097c9b483ba9f46a1a66b455a0.png)\n\nGiven the figure above, find the value of $w.$" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{w}{3}+{30}^{∘} & ={46}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{w}{3} & ={16}^{∘} \\\\ w & ={48}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3187f453-97bb-56c2-93bd-6746899eb80e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3187f453-97bb-56c2-93bd-6746899eb80e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3187f453-97bb-56c2-93bd-6746899eb80e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6ebc881e-ea45-5ad8-b704-3719442338fd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2a84106b-98b9-5583-8172-d48f0d697c51" "answer-b2c2c631-12ac-543f-85fa-ce5542797494" "answer-5f6ea231-8b01-5d66-b52a-1286684dcb9e" "answer-6ebc881e-ea45-5ad8-b704-3719442338fd" "answer-5d1e36ca-0303-51b3-88cd-367514f86088"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a84106b-98b9-5583-8172-d48f0d697c51" :answer/id #uuid "2a84106b-98b9-5583-8172-d48f0d697c51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{39}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2c2c631-12ac-543f-85fa-ce5542797494" :answer/id #uuid "b2c2c631-12ac-543f-85fa-ce5542797494" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f6ea231-8b01-5d66-b52a-1286684dcb9e" :answer/id #uuid "5f6ea231-8b01-5d66-b52a-1286684dcb9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ebc881e-ea45-5ad8-b704-3719442338fd" :answer/id #uuid "6ebc881e-ea45-5ad8-b704-3719442338fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d1e36ca-0303-51b3-88cd-367514f86088" :answer/id #uuid "5d1e36ca-0303-51b3-88cd-367514f86088" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1962d5a7-c8a1-5f9e-a8c4-8c270c23c07a" :question/id #uuid "1962d5a7-c8a1-5f9e-a8c4-8c270c23c07a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26284" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5089419745362aa42e024c04df35dd1c68d61d79b0f1ae52c97afd4e76843b0.png)\n\nGiven the figure above, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-{9}^{∘} & ={75}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & ={84}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={14}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d7fce25f-fdf9-5f82-9a1c-59dc1108e26f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d7fce25f-fdf9-5f82-9a1c-59dc1108e26f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7fce25f-fdf9-5f82-9a1c-59dc1108e26f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1a0feba6-10a5-52a9-a09b-083eeaf7ac6d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c8e3175-752e-537d-b8ff-86f5529a7875" "answer-f357346c-4884-5401-80de-a5f236e07ad9" "answer-1a0feba6-10a5-52a9-a09b-083eeaf7ac6d" "answer-73f4433e-9394-5dd7-9a7a-9e60b2990db2" "answer-7e60b62d-6012-5194-80da-228802678bf1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c8e3175-752e-537d-b8ff-86f5529a7875" :answer/id #uuid "3c8e3175-752e-537d-b8ff-86f5529a7875" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{12}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f357346c-4884-5401-80de-a5f236e07ad9" :answer/id #uuid "f357346c-4884-5401-80de-a5f236e07ad9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{13}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a0feba6-10a5-52a9-a09b-083eeaf7ac6d" :answer/id #uuid "1a0feba6-10a5-52a9-a09b-083eeaf7ac6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{14}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73f4433e-9394-5dd7-9a7a-9e60b2990db2" :answer/id #uuid "73f4433e-9394-5dd7-9a7a-9e60b2990db2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e60b62d-6012-5194-80da-228802678bf1" :answer/id #uuid "7e60b62d-6012-5194-80da-228802678bf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{16}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-804e107f-83a7-522e-91c4-4c6cf836e13f" :question/id #uuid "804e107f-83a7-522e-91c4-4c6cf836e13f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74745" :question/problem "![](images/ad/add18e4afb099c25b56b798ac69b8b02f7cee49949ef02b5971f71ffde0d7e5c.png)\n\nCompute the measures of the shaded angles in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-{32}^{∘} & =2x+{16}^{∘} \\\\ 4x-{32}^{∘} & ={16}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={48}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={12}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x={12}^{∘},$ we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $2x+{16}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n2({12}^{∘})+{16}^{∘}={40}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of ${40}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c6df7c54-8fba-571d-9d2a-8d68365d63ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c6df7c54-8fba-571d-9d2a-8d68365d63ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6df7c54-8fba-571d-9d2a-8d68365d63ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b5c31936-c89c-5fcc-9717-c9753724ebc3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6ea3652b-cdb3-5476-89f2-fe7577ee4139" "answer-a17fbd4a-7e1a-5e87-a6b7-3dc66dcadd47" "answer-b5c31936-c89c-5fcc-9717-c9753724ebc3" "answer-2133f9d2-c935-5203-8542-5540f16f2828" "answer-149c69aa-e028-560f-8266-d809972c424b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ea3652b-cdb3-5476-89f2-fe7577ee4139" :answer/id #uuid "6ea3652b-cdb3-5476-89f2-fe7577ee4139" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{36}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a17fbd4a-7e1a-5e87-a6b7-3dc66dcadd47" :answer/id #uuid "a17fbd4a-7e1a-5e87-a6b7-3dc66dcadd47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5c31936-c89c-5fcc-9717-c9753724ebc3" :answer/id #uuid "b5c31936-c89c-5fcc-9717-c9753724ebc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2133f9d2-c935-5203-8542-5540f16f2828" :answer/id #uuid "2133f9d2-c935-5203-8542-5540f16f2828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-149c69aa-e028-560f-8266-d809972c424b" :answer/id #uuid "149c69aa-e028-560f-8266-d809972c424b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-19c18e1c-068f-53bc-9c01-193db0152518" :question/id #uuid "19c18e1c-068f-53bc-9c01-193db0152518" :question/math-academy-id "q-114816" :question/problem "![](images/16/167f628be7f87d5fffa11fab9b9c8189880983a2364a992147e8e4bf84bbd13b.png)\n\nCompute the measures of the shaded angles in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8w-{24}^{∘} & =6w-{6}^{∘} \\\\ 2w-{24}^{∘} & =-{6}^{∘} \\\\ 2w & ={18}^{∘} \\\\ w & ={9}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $w={9}^{∘},$ we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $6w-{6}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n6({9}^{∘})-{6}^{∘}={48}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of ${48}^{∘}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d239139-54fa-55dc-952f-778d9d8bffd7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d239139-54fa-55dc-952f-778d9d8bffd7" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d239139-54fa-55dc-952f-778d9d8bffd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2bdcb6df-48c4-52ea-bdf7-9b4277eccfee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3bdd8f09-ba9d-5df5-b763-3bb8adbf08ca" "answer-702e16f9-92be-5088-ba74-cf3f9680ce57" "answer-4f5590db-b46d-5742-92ba-9cce38ee8869" "answer-2bdcb6df-48c4-52ea-bdf7-9b4277eccfee" "answer-a2538a7c-02a2-5964-81f0-1c7ccc207a45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bdd8f09-ba9d-5df5-b763-3bb8adbf08ca" :answer/id #uuid "3bdd8f09-ba9d-5df5-b763-3bb8adbf08ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{18}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-702e16f9-92be-5088-ba74-cf3f9680ce57" :answer/id #uuid "702e16f9-92be-5088-ba74-cf3f9680ce57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{24}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f5590db-b46d-5742-92ba-9cce38ee8869" :answer/id #uuid "4f5590db-b46d-5742-92ba-9cce38ee8869" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{42}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bdcb6df-48c4-52ea-bdf7-9b4277eccfee" :answer/id #uuid "2bdcb6df-48c4-52ea-bdf7-9b4277eccfee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{48}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2538a7c-02a2-5964-81f0-1c7ccc207a45" :answer/id #uuid "a2538a7c-02a2-5964-81f0-1c7ccc207a45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{54}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-97e7e9d8-dcf7-50ae-a8f6-45c7e2519058" :question/id #uuid "97e7e9d8-dcf7-50ae-a8f6-45c7e2519058" :question/math-academy-id "q-114829" :question/problem "![](images/ca/cafb9f7367b6f0f53a29e13e17f9b6c2cd6b516fa80afd1959842b52e176642c.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠POQ$ is a straight angle. Find $m∠QOS.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠POQ$ is straight, $m∠POQ={180}^{∘}.$ So by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm∠QOR+m∠ROP={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4w-{7}^{∘})+(6w-{13}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10w-{20}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10w & ={200}^{∘} \\\\ w & ={20}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $w={20}^{∘},$ we can calculate $m∠ROP$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm∠ROP=6({20}^{∘})-{13}^{∘}={107}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $∠QOS$ and $∠ROP$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm∠QOS=m∠ROP={107}^{∘}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be60585e-c426-51a1-bc8e-7cd292dae56b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be60585e-c426-51a1-bc8e-7cd292dae56b" :answer-field/id #uuid "be60585e-c426-51a1-bc8e-7cd292dae56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-66ee0543-c309-58a6-8132-2a4139cae805" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a760794f-75b5-525c-b70a-038d52cde7a6" "answer-66ee0543-c309-58a6-8132-2a4139cae805" "answer-377f3a1b-5e07-541c-a94f-b1c513567dd5" "answer-cdd29a49-1e2c-5857-9c7d-fcea54e05c3a" "answer-7e1d42f6-9820-56bf-b10c-55ede29b6213"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a760794f-75b5-525c-b70a-038d52cde7a6" :answer/id #uuid "a760794f-75b5-525c-b70a-038d52cde7a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{100}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66ee0543-c309-58a6-8132-2a4139cae805" :answer/id #uuid "66ee0543-c309-58a6-8132-2a4139cae805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{107}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-377f3a1b-5e07-541c-a94f-b1c513567dd5" :answer/id #uuid "377f3a1b-5e07-541c-a94f-b1c513567dd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{113}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdd29a49-1e2c-5857-9c7d-fcea54e05c3a" :answer/id #uuid "cdd29a49-1e2c-5857-9c7d-fcea54e05c3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{125}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e1d42f6-9820-56bf-b10c-55ede29b6213" :answer/id #uuid "7e1d42f6-9820-56bf-b10c-55ede29b6213" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{95}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5703c4ee-d713-54f9-901a-aaa46a569aaf" :question/id #uuid "5703c4ee-d713-54f9-901a-aaa46a569aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-114834" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc5840723aad59a7632bc099e8f410df98234cf50f3532c0676f8bf96f673040.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠SOT$ is a straight angle. Find $m∠TOV.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠SOT$ is straight, $m∠SOT={180}^{∘}.$ So by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm∠SOU+m∠UOT={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2z+{60}^{∘})+(3z+{10}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5z+{70}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 5z & ={110}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={22}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $z={22}^{∘},$ we can calculate $m∠SOU$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm∠SOU=2({22}^{∘})+{60}^{∘}={104}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $∠SOU$ and $∠TOV$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm∠TOV=m∠SOU={104}^{∘}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9f87137d-ca0a-5913-9d5e-eb858093c6e6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f87137d-ca0a-5913-9d5e-eb858093c6e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f87137d-ca0a-5913-9d5e-eb858093c6e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cc298b70-5db5-5c84-aa8d-ca401cc22d97" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc298b70-5db5-5c84-aa8d-ca401cc22d97" "answer-882325ab-a3ac-5ba7-8b01-0b170b1f0e1c" "answer-578b62f5-332b-5303-a97f-311f1eff667c" "answer-9f43dded-448e-5577-8e89-bd75aaa50359" "answer-c44a8416-dd42-5e16-be10-97a770987c1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc298b70-5db5-5c84-aa8d-ca401cc22d97" :answer/id #uuid "cc298b70-5db5-5c84-aa8d-ca401cc22d97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{104}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-882325ab-a3ac-5ba7-8b01-0b170b1f0e1c" :answer/id #uuid "882325ab-a3ac-5ba7-8b01-0b170b1f0e1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{110}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-578b62f5-332b-5303-a97f-311f1eff667c" :answer/id #uuid "578b62f5-332b-5303-a97f-311f1eff667c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{115}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f43dded-448e-5577-8e89-bd75aaa50359" :answer/id #uuid "9f43dded-448e-5577-8e89-bd75aaa50359" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{118}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c44a8416-dd42-5e16-be10-97a770987c1f" :answer/id #uuid "c44a8416-dd42-5e16-be10-97a770987c1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{95}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f79072b2-718e-5891-9f14-a1fea25a8bca" :question/id #uuid "f79072b2-718e-5891-9f14-a1fea25a8bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-26290" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d2a4e7a727bd5643187203bcfc30e4cc8369e1e1fe53eea4d11b50cfb405622.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $m∠1={58}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={122}^{∘}.$ Determine the value of $m∠3-m∠2.$" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1 & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m∠1={58}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={122}^{∘},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{58}^{∘} & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & ={122}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠3-m∠2 & ={122}^{∘}-{58}^{∘} \\\\  & ={64}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b41d4023-9f62-5631-9156-ee1b60a613dc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b41d4023-9f62-5631-9156-ee1b60a613dc" :answer-field/id #uuid "b41d4023-9f62-5631-9156-ee1b60a613dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24fdcc23-9585-58da-90d0-ece5fce06da8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-819895b7-079d-567f-bde9-93cf2b2972c0" "answer-f14d4356-27f8-58af-ad21-89505078c4bf" "answer-80223d72-b965-5832-9820-d80f40ea2176" "answer-c2ac99a9-90ce-5068-af92-0cdb4162cb69" "answer-24fdcc23-9585-58da-90d0-ece5fce06da8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-819895b7-079d-567f-bde9-93cf2b2972c0" :answer/id #uuid "819895b7-079d-567f-bde9-93cf2b2972c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f14d4356-27f8-58af-ad21-89505078c4bf" :answer/id #uuid "f14d4356-27f8-58af-ad21-89505078c4bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{55}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80223d72-b965-5832-9820-d80f40ea2176" :answer/id #uuid "80223d72-b965-5832-9820-d80f40ea2176" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{58}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2ac99a9-90ce-5068-af92-0cdb4162cb69" :answer/id #uuid "c2ac99a9-90ce-5068-af92-0cdb4162cb69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24fdcc23-9585-58da-90d0-ece5fce06da8" :answer/id #uuid "24fdcc23-9585-58da-90d0-ece5fce06da8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{64}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b19a18fa-549e-563d-980d-10ad4fe9bdeb" :question/id #uuid "b19a18fa-549e-563d-980d-10ad4fe9bdeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-26295" :question/problem "![](images/28/283adada8b7da0b7f481e0975a6b3b97c989e69fd63e0577839d00b6ee69a22b.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $m∠1={35}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={111}^{∘}.$ Determine the value of $m∠2+m∠3.$" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1 & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m∠1={35}^{∘}$ and $m∠4={111}^{∘},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{35}^{∘} & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & ={111}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠2+m∠3 & ={35}^{∘}+{111}^{∘} \\\\  & ={146}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-717cb0e3-7172-5bf0-8ea1-e1eda72c6ca2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-717cb0e3-7172-5bf0-8ea1-e1eda72c6ca2" :answer-field/id #uuid "717cb0e3-7172-5bf0-8ea1-e1eda72c6ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1cbc1069-9183-5c8d-935b-5fff97e092d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ade94a75-98a0-59ed-8a25-47900492f095" "answer-1cbc1069-9183-5c8d-935b-5fff97e092d7" "answer-110ff2f5-88f1-5823-bb7b-c612d3a1ff8d" "answer-4635fe22-0407-5309-8c9f-af59ddb32c07" "answer-f46477e4-f220-5805-a013-9248858e57ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ade94a75-98a0-59ed-8a25-47900492f095" :answer/id #uuid "ade94a75-98a0-59ed-8a25-47900492f095" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{126}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cbc1069-9183-5c8d-935b-5fff97e092d7" :answer/id #uuid "1cbc1069-9183-5c8d-935b-5fff97e092d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{146}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-110ff2f5-88f1-5823-bb7b-c612d3a1ff8d" :answer/id #uuid "110ff2f5-88f1-5823-bb7b-c612d3a1ff8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{176}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4635fe22-0407-5309-8c9f-af59ddb32c07" :answer/id #uuid "4635fe22-0407-5309-8c9f-af59ddb32c07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{190}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f46477e4-f220-5805-a013-9248858e57ed" :answer/id #uuid "f46477e4-f220-5805-a013-9248858e57ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{192}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2571ba1e-66f6-5370-ac1a-d3c46be963c3" :question/id #uuid "2571ba1e-66f6-5370-ac1a-d3c46be963c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-171786" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb9043c3082f373a92aaf6b8d4289e950d645eef7325cf5a3abc04ef83e31a7c.png)\n\nIn the diagram shown above, $m∠1={122}^{∘}$ and $m∠3={67}^{∘}.$ Then $m∠2=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}$ and $m∠4=$\n{{field-2}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1 & =m∠2, \\\\ m∠3 & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $m∠1={122}^{∘}$ and $m∠3={67}^{∘},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{122}^{∘} & =m∠2, \\\\ {67}^{∘} & =m∠4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d30f65f-f7bc-5ce0-af55-c5a1beb40838" "answer-field-159868c0-14b1-57b6-816a-3e596621b328"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3d30f65f-f7bc-5ce0-af55-c5a1beb40838" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d30f65f-f7bc-5ce0-af55-c5a1beb40838" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3d0960af-476c-5200-b5e5-db9a94a707b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d0960af-476c-5200-b5e5-db9a94a707b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d0960af-476c-5200-b5e5-db9a94a707b7" :answer/id #uuid "3d0960af-476c-5200-b5e5-db9a94a707b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "122" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-159868c0-14b1-57b6-816a-3e596621b328" :answer-field/id #uuid "159868c0-14b1-57b6-816a-3e596621b328" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5ff897a6-0d24-528b-96b9-37b6a6132f4c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ff897a6-0d24-528b-96b9-37b6a6132f4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ff897a6-0d24-528b-96b9-37b6a6132f4c" :answer/id #uuid "5ff897a6-0d24-528b-96b9-37b6a6132f4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "67" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111838 :tutorial/content "When two lines meet at a point, they create two pairs of opposite angles. The angles in each pair are called **vertical angles**.\n\nVertical angles are *always* congruent.\n\n![](images/0d/0d5b2895ae18beb7242ef666af08e5b8e3490880de99aec8d993b02f31a9fcf4.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 3$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent. \n\nLikewise, $\\angle 2$ and $\\angle 4$ are vertical angles and are therefore congruent. \n\nSo, we have $\\angle 1 \\cong \\angle 3$ and $\\angle 2 \\cong \\angle 4.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110862 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1c7906bc-d806-5034-89ba-962181be4d73"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198089 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/da/da2ae479bd2bb629add35ffd9f0a66bc96256ad380b7f61789893d9bc9be6ef0.svg)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/95/955f504032fc42c502d4cd1bb8f68645930cdf2b4a08a48e80eea2ac6214acc9.svg)  \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = 5,$ we get \n\n![](images/2f/2f70f885f2773dc48443efba4c673d6d19f443b4fc1ad8f135e2cab95fad21ea.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198088 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/e2/e207d971031273136f2c8ddc4e4f4ea462a4f7d8aa9c893b0973101a0e2e24f4.svg)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/ea/eab2969eff48fccb1fc2c4e91181e83b71707f80d1e315e07293aeb5b9011704.svg)   \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is already normalized ($\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|=1$), we conclude that,   ![](images/98/98be1156a6de649bcd1aee6f38b2c71447089e477b50150795517d5704e3fa25.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e463920-832a-54ce-b343-95505e40d97a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198090 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n![](images/ef/ef4c1994a6c3980934ab8b95a92be32293c434fa84a0ffb70dc82cf3198367b2.svg)\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_1^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{4^2}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$\\qquad$ $y_1={x_1} \\quad$ and $\\quad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{4},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n![](images/df/dfa724469e02e9851ec5dc895fe7dda810836745d3a1781a8c60f01d09fbd32b.svg) \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}= Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 \\\\ 32 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimise $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}16-\\lambda  & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )(16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-32\\lambda -768 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +16) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}} = -16.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=-16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-16I) & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{aligned}]+16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-16I))\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/49/49d16ecc1b8c785d665bd961f24f5627f18fceec2a74dee5b2b1f4c39f2864a8.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/18/18d7c92aca6dc096a79cf0ae8ea6ef99e058da58481689af353f430e726b69a5.svg) \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186389622 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269174 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1))x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198559 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1c7906bc-d806-5034-89ba-962181be4d73" :question/id #uuid "1c7906bc-d806-5034-89ba-962181be4d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-170402" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $0.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe  maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dd63ecac-8751-5520-937a-d3c900eac80e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dd63ecac-8751-5520-937a-d3c900eac80e" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd63ecac-8751-5520-937a-d3c900eac80e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c0497484-5b80-5f14-9367-302334efa418" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96521b38-e0b7-58e6-b1db-eeedf6a34c29" "answer-c0497484-5b80-5f14-9367-302334efa418" "answer-1d9e2ab8-f0d5-5abe-b74e-db6a46609bb7" "answer-f9b05c42-2662-52e1-be4f-95016554eeb3" "answer-208b060c-d7f6-5417-9e03-796577024e7f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96521b38-e0b7-58e6-b1db-eeedf6a34c29" :answer/id #uuid "96521b38-e0b7-58e6-b1db-eeedf6a34c29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0497484-5b80-5f14-9367-302334efa418" :answer/id #uuid "c0497484-5b80-5f14-9367-302334efa418" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d9e2ab8-f0d5-5abe-b74e-db6a46609bb7" :answer/id #uuid "1d9e2ab8-f0d5-5abe-b74e-db6a46609bb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9b05c42-2662-52e1-be4f-95016554eeb3" :answer/id #uuid "f9b05c42-2662-52e1-be4f-95016554eeb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-208b060c-d7f6-5417-9e03-796577024e7f" :answer/id #uuid "208b060c-d7f6-5417-9e03-796577024e7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198562 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1e463920-832a-54ce-b343-95505e40d97a" :question/id #uuid "1e463920-832a-54ce-b343-95505e40d97a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170527" :question/problem "The minimum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-9{x}_{2}^{2},$ when ${x}_{1}^{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}=9,$ is $-18.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=3$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=1$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}={x}_{2},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the minimum value of the quadratic form is $-18,$ we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{min}=-18.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-18:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-18)I & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -18 \\\\ -18 & -9\\end{aligned}]+18[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}36 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-18)I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4ec5a8e-4792-5032-a904-759d0e616ab6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4ec5a8e-4792-5032-a904-759d0e616ab6" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ec5a8e-4792-5032-a904-759d0e616ab6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d70b4dc0-8536-581a-8bd5-450e119abdcf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eb3d2221-dc50-598c-ba5a-1cb5379b5184" "answer-1c4c2ff5-edba-514a-87a6-58daf1e367d1" "answer-f99577b0-7bae-5456-97fc-603ae4a89241" "answer-5b0214e5-26b9-58f4-866b-8bc611fb2ef7" "answer-d70b4dc0-8536-581a-8bd5-450e119abdcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb3d2221-dc50-598c-ba5a-1cb5379b5184" :answer/id #uuid "eb3d2221-dc50-598c-ba5a-1cb5379b5184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c4c2ff5-edba-514a-87a6-58daf1e367d1" :answer/id #uuid "1c4c2ff5-edba-514a-87a6-58daf1e367d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f99577b0-7bae-5456-97fc-603ae4a89241" :answer/id #uuid "f99577b0-7bae-5456-97fc-603ae4a89241" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b0214e5-26b9-58f4-866b-8bc611fb2ef7" :answer/id #uuid "5b0214e5-26b9-58f4-866b-8bc611fb2ef7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d70b4dc0-8536-581a-8bd5-450e119abdcf" :answer/id #uuid "d70b4dc0-8536-581a-8bd5-450e119abdcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198684 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $40,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=40.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=40:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-40I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}]-40[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-40I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186410197 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $25,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=25.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=25:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-25I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186378962 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}36-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (36-\\lambda )(36-\\lambda )-144 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-72\\lambda +1\\,152 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=24.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199515 :tutorial/content "If $A$ is the matrix corresponding to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ then:  \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}$ of $A,$ and the maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}$ of $A,$ and the minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nSuppose we're given that the maximum value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-10\\: \\! x_1x_2+x_2^2\n$$\n\n \n\nwhen $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is $6.$ Let's use this to find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}$ where this maximum value is attained. \n\nSo first, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/a6/a6165f41eb43e8f6e8fd4399f1c428f73481de9d4b5ec41293f3ec0e4f062fc6.svg)\n\nNext, we find the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/30/30699c4f0abcb099a6f7a76cbd76674757c3f7de313e034d0870d1fce27fdd8c.svg) \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n![](images/ef/ef14ea5facf5010b99721cf915f44162dfa52e089508fb78c2d02d9aad66f1a4.svg)  \n\nLet's check that this is indeed the vector we were looking for: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{\\text{max}}) & ={(\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}-10(\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}})(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})+{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{10}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199516 :tutorial/content "Suppose we're given that the maximum value of the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_1x_2+x_2^2,\n$$\n\n over the ellipse $4x_1^2+x_2^2=4$ equals $5.$ How can we find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which this maximum is attained?\n\nWe can find this vector by applying a suitable change of variable. This change of variable should transform our problem into one where we need to maximize a related quadratic form $Q'$ on the unit circle. Let's see how this is done.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/7b/7b1fd813963e16887c57d6c10be1bfe288e3209fd3a02ca6e6236b7261feb7b1.svg)\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n{x_1^2}+ \\dfrac{x_2^2}{2^2}=1\n$$\n\nwhich  represents an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=2$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$$\ny_1={x_1}, \\qquad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{2},\n$$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n![](images/80/80f8e821a60b63dde0f314460adfd31faca5d82315243fb2a3ebdec75bc61538.svg) \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}=Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in the new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the goal is to maximize $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ which we do using our usual methods.  \n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\max} = 5.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-5I)\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/4b/4b51afece6e21c2fe073a5346557f42f9f03ae3400407d18ed6af3af8f32e310.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n![](images/ce/ce571338fc1d83f0b0892a356bca56073c096a1f5191a21a4baef56b6d5a399f.svg) \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199547 :tutorial/content "To complete the picture, let's prove the main theorem of this lesson:\n\n*The maximum value of a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x},$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$* \n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n*The maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$*\n\n**Note:** We'll show only the first part (about the maximum value) since a similar argument can be used to prove the second part (about the minimum value).\n\nLet $A$ be a real $n \\times n$ symmetric matrix corresponding to a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)\\mathbin.$ As a result, there must be an *orthonormal* matrix $P$ such that \n\n- $D=P^{-1}AP=P^TAP,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\n- The diagonal entries of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A$. Without loss of generality, we can assume that the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1 \\geq \\lambda_2 \\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n,$ where $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}} = \\lambda_1.$\n\n- The columns $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\mathbf{u}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{u}_n$ of $P$ form an orthonormal set of unit eigenvectors of $A.$ So, $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}} = \\mathbf{u}_1.$\n\nTake $\\mathbf x=P\\mathbf y.$ Since, $P$ is orthonormal, we have\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf x\\| = \\|P \\mathbf y \\| = \\|\\mathbf y\\|\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf x^T\\!A\\mathbf x = (P\\mathbf y)^T\\!A(P\\mathbf y) = \\mathbf{y}^T (P^T\\!AP) \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{y}^T D \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the following:\n\n- $\\|\\mathbf x\\| =1 \\Longleftrightarrow \\|\\mathbf y\\| =1$ for all $\\mathbf y$ such that $\\mathbf x=P\\mathbf y$\n\n- Restricted to the unit sphere of $\\Bbb R^n,$ the images of the quadratic forms $Q(\\mathbf x)$  and $Q'(\\mathbf y)= \\mathbf y^T D\\mathbf y$ coincide: \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x: \\: \\: \\|\\mathbf x\\|=1\\}= \\{\\mathbf y^TD\\mathbf y: \\: \\: \\|\\mathbf y\\|=1\\}  \\subset \\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNow, for any unit vector $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={Q}^{′}(y) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{1}{y}_{1}^{2}+{\\lambda }_{2}{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{\\lambda }_{n}{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{1}^{2}+{\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}({y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{y}_{n}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\\,‖y{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that  that \n\n$$\nM = \\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\max \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{y} \\| = 1 \\} \\le \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, if we take ![](images/53/53a051ea36059c9222e717c65adc6531bc313c9807d44a39b262433acfdcbc5c.svg) then $Q(P\\mathbf{e}_1) = Q'(\\mathbf{e}_1) = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$ So, we conclude that $M = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\nFinally, since $\\mathbf{u}_1 = P\\mathbf{e}_1$ we have that \n$Q(\\mathbf u_1) =  \\mathbf e_1^T D \\mathbf e_1 = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}.$ \nTherefore, the maximum value is attained at $\\mathbf u_{\\textrm{max}}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198510 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179640 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4e9751ba-f740-5aab-b93b-fcf4271231ee" "question-3e267a47-5572-5324-88e0-0916a71d377e" "question-f0804f3e-fd28-56db-8f75-05c9b0dc9357"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4e9751ba-f740-5aab-b93b-fcf4271231ee" :question/id #uuid "4e9751ba-f740-5aab-b93b-fcf4271231ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-101832" :question/problem "If $1-3fg=2f-1,$ then $f=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $f$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $1$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-3fg & =2f-1 \\\\ 1-3fg-1 & =2f-1-1 \\\\ -3fg & =2f-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $2f$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3fg & =2f-2 \\\\ -3fg-2f & =2f-2-2f \\\\ -3fg-2f & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $f$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $f$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $f$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3fg-2f & =-2 \\\\ f(-3g-2) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(-3g-2)$ to isolate $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3g-2) & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{f(-3g-2)}{-3g-2} & =\\frac{-2}{-3g-2} \\\\ \\frac{f\\cancel{(-3g-2)}}{\\cancel{-3g-2}} & =\\frac{-2}{-(3g+2)} \\\\ f & =\\frac{2}{3g+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $3g+2\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6205bf4c-4b47-54d9-8d23-7d84c77677a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6205bf4c-4b47-54d9-8d23-7d84c77677a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "6205bf4c-4b47-54d9-8d23-7d84c77677a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-de9c99ca-2c92-5c32-84c7-e35e8e18c3ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43633fbd-1329-596c-93e7-dc24c7abbb5b" "answer-014429d8-b013-59f0-9ef9-dceaaf7c1a8f" "answer-37e76a90-f6df-5aa4-9537-258eef7d84ca" "answer-de9c99ca-2c92-5c32-84c7-e35e8e18c3ed" "answer-57feefc7-e907-5471-a117-f1cad8eea4eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43633fbd-1329-596c-93e7-dc24c7abbb5b" :answer/id #uuid "43633fbd-1329-596c-93e7-dc24c7abbb5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3g+2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-014429d8-b013-59f0-9ef9-dceaaf7c1a8f" :answer/id #uuid "014429d8-b013-59f0-9ef9-dceaaf7c1a8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2g-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37e76a90-f6df-5aa4-9537-258eef7d84ca" :answer/id #uuid "37e76a90-f6df-5aa4-9537-258eef7d84ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2g+1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de9c99ca-2c92-5c32-84c7-e35e8e18c3ed" :answer/id #uuid "de9c99ca-2c92-5c32-84c7-e35e8e18c3ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3g+2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57feefc7-e907-5471-a117-f1cad8eea4eb" :answer/id #uuid "57feefc7-e907-5471-a117-f1cad8eea4eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3g+2}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e267a47-5572-5324-88e0-0916a71d377e" :question/id #uuid "3e267a47-5572-5324-88e0-0916a71d377e" :question/math-academy-id "q-133848" :question/problem "If $11fg+7=f+4g,$ then $g=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $g$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $7$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11fg+7 & =f+4g \\\\ 11fg+7-7 & =f+4g-7 \\\\ 11fg & =f+4g-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $4g$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11fg & =f+4g-7 \\\\ 11fg-4g & =f+4g-7-4g \\\\ 11fg-4g & =f-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $g$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $g$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $g$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11fg-4g & =f-7 \\\\ g(11f-4) & =f-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(11f-4)$ to isolate $g:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(11f-4) & =f-7 \\\\ \\frac{g(11f-4)}{11f-4} & =\\frac{f-7}{11f-4} \\\\ \\frac{g\\cancel{(11f-4)}}{\\cancel{11f-4}} & =\\frac{f-7}{11f-4} \\\\ g & =\\frac{f-7}{11f-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $11f-4\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ef959e6c-abd2-51af-b36c-77958fb6ba0c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ef959e6c-abd2-51af-b36c-77958fb6ba0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef959e6c-abd2-51af-b36c-77958fb6ba0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4bd79e1c-2bd0-5b12-8d18-2f49a2dfbe20" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c87c5036-7ee4-5a07-b18a-2234e9e8e8d3" "answer-272872da-d0aa-5122-b591-cb63db2b1fd4" "answer-16558bc4-6554-5913-bf18-952e26ae16e4" "answer-27236521-7973-56f9-87e0-9e36f16e6909" "answer-4bd79e1c-2bd0-5b12-8d18-2f49a2dfbe20"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c87c5036-7ee4-5a07-b18a-2234e9e8e8d3" :answer/id #uuid "c87c5036-7ee4-5a07-b18a-2234e9e8e8d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7f}{11f-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-272872da-d0aa-5122-b591-cb63db2b1fd4" :answer/id #uuid "272872da-d0aa-5122-b591-cb63db2b1fd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{f+11}{7f-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16558bc4-6554-5913-bf18-952e26ae16e4" :answer/id #uuid "16558bc4-6554-5913-bf18-952e26ae16e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{f+7}{11f-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27236521-7973-56f9-87e0-9e36f16e6909" :answer/id #uuid "27236521-7973-56f9-87e0-9e36f16e6909" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{f-11}{7f-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bd79e1c-2bd0-5b12-8d18-2f49a2dfbe20" :answer/id #uuid "4bd79e1c-2bd0-5b12-8d18-2f49a2dfbe20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{f-7}{11f-4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0804f3e-fd28-56db-8f75-05c9b0dc9357" :question/id #uuid "f0804f3e-fd28-56db-8f75-05c9b0dc9357" :question/math-academy-id "q-215770" :question/problem "If $5pq=1+8p,$ then $p=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $p$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we subtract $8p$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5pq & =1+8p \\\\ 5pq-8p & =1+8p-8p \\\\ 5pq-8p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $p$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $p$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nNext, we factor out $p$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5pq-8p & =1 \\\\ (5q-8)p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(5q-8)$ to isolate $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5q-8)p & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(5q-8)p}{5q-8} & =\\frac{1}{5q-8} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(5q-8)}p}{\\cancel{5q-8}} & =\\frac{1}{5q-8} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{5q-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $5q-8\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f144f7c-581f-5a2e-8b69-cd844d062564"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f144f7c-581f-5a2e-8b69-cd844d062564" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f144f7c-581f-5a2e-8b69-cd844d062564" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b902175b-6032-5abd-b89d-ba04a4e007bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b902175b-6032-5abd-b89d-ba04a4e007bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b902175b-6032-5abd-b89d-ba04a4e007bc" :answer/id #uuid "b902175b-6032-5abd-b89d-ba04a4e007bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5q-8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179641 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7040b2b2-59d1-533f-a002-d851f289f8ef"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7040b2b2-59d1-533f-a002-d851f289f8ef" :question/id #uuid "7040b2b2-59d1-533f-a002-d851f289f8ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-215794" :question/problem "If $\\frac{4pq+5}{5}=\\frac{4-3q}{3},$ then $q=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate all terms with $q$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we first cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4pq+5}{5} & =\\frac{4-3q}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (4pq+5) & =5\\cdot (4-3q) \\\\ 12pq+15 & =20-15q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $15q$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12pq+15 & =20-15q \\\\ 12pq+15+15q & =20-15q+15q \\\\ 12pq+15+15q & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $15$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12pq+15+15q & =20 \\\\ 12pq+15+15q-15 & =20-15 \\\\ 12pq+15q & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $q$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $q$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $q$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12pq+15q & =5 \\\\ (12p+15)q & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(12p+15)$ to isolate $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12p+15)q & =5 \\\\ \\frac{(12p+15)q}{12p+15} & =\\frac{5}{12p+15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(12p+15)}q}{\\cancel{12p+15}} & =\\frac{5}{12p+15} \\\\ q & =\\frac{5}{12p+15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $12p+15\\ne 0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b2ac98ce-4a52-56e4-b996-e489a99ca79a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b2ac98ce-4a52-56e4-b996-e489a99ca79a" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2ac98ce-4a52-56e4-b996-e489a99ca79a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-35f1e89f-7b21-5fb2-aeee-c538c33cdaff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35f1e89f-7b21-5fb2-aeee-c538c33cdaff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35f1e89f-7b21-5fb2-aeee-c538c33cdaff" :answer/id #uuid "35f1e89f-7b21-5fb2-aeee-c538c33cdaff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12p+15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b35e3a1b-0166-5516-8aa0-8b1121392270" :activity/id #uuid "b35e3a1b-0166-5516-8aa0-8b1121392270" :activity/title "Solving Linear Equations With Unknown Coefficients by Factoring" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049734 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-c1b90b91-ff5b-52bb-aa5d-786d8f31778b" "step-a17be8bf-d2ce-5f25-87af-9e999ef8b896" "step-393d58c8-b803-5dd1-8f8b-d2a9adb7e4fd" "step-e1e772cc-8c6c-589d-97d1-c78fa38dd9e0"] :activity/first-step "step-c1b90b